\label{ch:type-checking}

Type checking and type inference stand at the crossroads of programming language theory and compiler implementation. A type checker verifies that a program respects the constraints imposed by its type system; a type inferencer goes further, reconstructing the types that the programmer chose not to annotate. Together, these two activities form the backbone of static analysis in modern compilers, and their design profoundly shapes both the expressiveness of a language and the quality of error messages its users receive.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of type checking and type inference from first principles. We begin by recasting type checking as a constraint generation problem, then present the unification algorithm that lies at the heart of most type inference procedures. We proceed to bidirectional type checking, a technique that elegantly handles systems where full inference is undecidable or impractical, and close with a survey of implementation strategies used in production compilers.

