%% Section 43.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Type Checking as Constraint Generation}\label{sec:constraint-generation}

The traditional presentation of type checking uses syntax-directed rules that compute a type for each expression in a single top-down pass. While this presentation is clear, it conflates two conceptually distinct phases: \emph{gathering} the constraints that types must satisfy, and \emph{solving} those constraints. Separating these phases yields a more modular architecture and, as we shall see, enables powerful generalizations.

\subsection{The Constraint-Based View}\label{sec:constraint-view}

In the constraint-based approach, type checking proceeds in two stages:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Constraint generation.} Traverse the abstract syntax tree, introducing fresh type variables for unknown types and emitting constraints that relate them.
  \item \textbf{Constraint solving.} Find an assignment of concrete types to the type variables that satisfies all constraints simultaneously.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Type Constraint]\label{def:type-constraint}
A \emph{type constraint} over a set of type variables $\mathcal{V}$ and a set of type constructors $\mathcal{C}$ is a predicate of the form $\tau_1 = \tau_2$, where $\tau_1, \tau_2$ are types built from $\mathcal{V} \cup \mathcal{C}$. A \emph{constraint system} $C$ is a finite set of such constraints.
\end{definition}

A \emph{solution} to a constraint system $C$ is a substitution $\sigma : \mathcal{V} \to \mathrm{Types}(\mathcal{C})$ such that for every $(\tau_1 = \tau_2) \in C$, we have $\sigma(\tau_1) = \sigma(\tau_2)$.

\subsection{Constraint Generation Rules}\label{sec:constraint-gen-rules}

Consider a simply-typed lambda calculus extended with type variables. We define a judgment $\Gamma \vdash e \rightsquigarrow \tau \mid C$ meaning ``under environment $\Gamma$, expression $e$ has type $\tau$ subject to constraint set $C$.''

\begin{semrules}{Constraint Generation}
  \typeaxiom{Var}{\Gamma, x : \tau \vdash x \rightsquigarrow \tau \mid \emptyset} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs}{\Gamma, x : \alpha \vdash e \rightsquigarrow \tau \mid C \quad \alpha \text{ fresh}}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x.\, e \rightsquigarrow \alpha \to \tau \mid C} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 \rightsquigarrow \tau_1 \mid C_1 \quad
                  \Gamma \vdash e_2 \rightsquigarrow \tau_2 \mid C_2 \quad
                  \alpha \text{ fresh}}
            {\Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 \rightsquigarrow \alpha \mid C_1 \cup C_2 \cup \{\tau_1 = \tau_2 \to \alpha\}} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Let}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 \rightsquigarrow \tau_1 \mid C_1 \quad
                  \Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e_2 \rightsquigarrow \tau_2 \mid C_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{let}\; x = e_1\; \mathtt{in}\; e_2 \rightsquigarrow \tau_2 \mid C_1 \cup C_2}
\end{semrules}

\begin{example}[Constraint generation for application]\label{ex:constraint-app}
Consider the expression $(\lambda f.\, \lambda x.\, f\; x)\; (\lambda y.\, y)$. Constraint generation proceeds as follows:

For the argument $\lambda y.\, y$, we introduce a fresh variable $\alpha$ and obtain type $\alpha \to \alpha$ with empty constraints.

For the function $\lambda f.\, \lambda x.\, f\; x$, we introduce fresh variables $\beta, \gamma$ for $f$ and $x$ respectively, and a fresh $\delta$ for the result of the application $f\; x$. This yields the constraint $\beta = \gamma \to \delta$ and type $\beta \to (\gamma \to \delta)$.

The outer application generates the constraint:
\[
  \beta \to (\gamma \to \delta) = (\alpha \to \alpha) \to \epsilon
\]
where $\epsilon$ is fresh. Solving yields $\beta = \alpha \to \alpha$, $\epsilon = \gamma \to \delta$, $\gamma = \alpha$, $\delta = \alpha$, giving the final type $(\alpha \to \alpha) \to \alpha \to \alpha$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Soundness and Completeness}\label{sec:soundness-completeness}

The constraint-based approach is sound and complete with respect to the standard type system, in the following sense:

\begin{theorem}[Equivalence of Constraint-Based and Declarative Typing]\label{thm:constraint-equiv}
For all $\Gamma$ and $e$, there exists $\tau$ and $C$ such that $\Gamma \vdash e \rightsquigarrow \tau \mid C$ and a solution $\sigma$ of $C$ with $\sigma(\tau) = \tau'$ if and only if $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on the structure of $e$. The key case is application, where the constraint $\tau_1 = \tau_2 \to \alpha$ ensures that the function's type is compatible with the argument's type. The substitution $\sigma$ resolves all type variables to concrete types, yielding exactly the derivations of the declarative system. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Advantages of the Constraint-Based Approach}\label{sec:constraint-advantages}

The separation of constraint generation from solving offers several benefits:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Modularity.} The constraint generator can be written independently of the solver. New type features (records, variants, overloading) add constraints without modifying the solver.
  \item \textbf{Error reporting.} Unsolved constraints can be analyzed to produce informative error messages, identifying the source location of conflicting requirements.
  \item \textbf{Extensibility.} Type classes, row polymorphism, and other extensions can be expressed as additional constraint forms handled by specialized solvers.
  \item \textbf{Incremental checking.} When a program is edited, only the affected constraints need regeneration, enabling fast incremental type checking in IDEs.
\end{itemize}

