\section{The Unification Algorithm}\label{sec:unification}

Constraint solving for simple type equations reduces to the problem of \emph{unification}: given a set of equations between terms, find a substitution that makes both sides of every equation identical. Unification was introduced by Herbrand~\cite{herbrand1931} and formalized by Robinson~\cite{robinson1965}.

\subsection{First-Order Unification}\label{sec:first-order-unification}

\begin{definition}[Unification Problem]\label{def:unification}
A \emph{unification problem} is a finite set of equations $E = \{s_1 = t_1, \ldots, s_n = t_n\}$ where each $s_i, t_i$ is a first-order term built from variables $\mathcal{V}$ and function symbols $\mathcal{F}$. A \emph{unifier} is a substitution $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(s_i) = \sigma(t_i)$ for all $i$. A unifier $\sigma$ is \emph{most general} (an MGU) if for every other unifier $\theta$, there exists a substitution $\rho$ such that $\theta = \rho \circ \sigma$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Existence and Uniqueness of MGU]\label{thm:mgu}
If a unification problem $E$ has a unifier, then it has a most general unifier, unique up to renaming of variables.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Robinson's Unification Algorithm}\label{sec:robinson-algorithm}

The classical algorithm, due to Robinson~\cite{robinson1965}, works by iteratively simplifying the equation set through a process called \emph{syntactic decomposition}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Robinson's Unification Algorithm}\label{alg:unify}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Unify}{$E$}
  \State $\sigma \gets \mathrm{id}$
  \While{$E \neq \emptyset$}
    \State Remove an equation $s = t$ from $E$
    \If{$s = t$}
      \State \Comment{Trivial: skip}
    \ElsIf{$s$ is a variable $x$ and $x \notin \mathrm{vars}(t)$}
      \State $\sigma \gets \sigma \circ \{t/x\}$
      \State $E \gets \{r[t/x] = u[t/x] \mid (r = u) \in E\}$
    \ElsIf{$t$ is a variable $x$ and $x \notin \mathrm{vars}(s)$}
      \State $\sigma \gets \sigma \circ \{s/x\}$
      \State $E \gets \{r[s/x] = u[s/x] \mid (r = u) \in E\}$
    \ElsIf{$s = f(s_1, \ldots, s_n)$ and $t = f(t_1, \ldots, t_n)$}
      \State $E \gets E \cup \{s_1 = t_1, \ldots, s_n = t_n\}$
    \Else
      \State \Return \textbf{fail} \Comment{Occurs check or clash}
    \EndIf
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return $\sigma$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{The Occurs Check}\label{sec:occurs-check}

The \emph{occurs check} tests whether a variable $x$ appears within a term $t$. If $x \in \mathrm{vars}(t)$, then the equation $x = t$ has no finite solution (it would require an infinite term). This check is essential for correctness.

\begin{definition}[Occurs Check]\label{def:occurs-check}
The predicate $\mathrm{occurs}(x, t)$ is defined recursively:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathrm{occurs}(x, x) = \mathrm{true}$
  \item $\mathrm{occurs}(x, y) = \mathrm{false}$ for $y \neq x$
  \item $\mathrm{occurs}(x, f(t_1, \ldots, t_n)) = \bigvee_{i=1}^{n} \mathrm{occurs}(x, t_i)$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{insight}
The occurs check is the single most common source of bugs in naive unification implementations. Many production systems omit it for performance (notably early Prolog systems), accepting the risk of unsound infinite types. Modern type checkers universally include it.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Efficient Unification}\label{sec:efficient-unification}

Robinson's algorithm has worst-case complexity $O(n^2)$ due to repeated application of substitutions. Paterson and Wegman~\cite{paterson1978} presented an $O(n \alpha(n))$ algorithm using a union-find data structure with path compression, where $\alpha$ is the inverse Ackermann function.

The key insight is to represent terms as directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) rather than trees, sharing common subexpressions. When two variables are unified, their equivalence classes are merged in the union-find structure, and the DAG edges are updated accordingly.

