\subsection{Unification with Partial Information}\label{sec:partial-unification}

In type inference, we often encounter \emph{conditional} constraints: ``if $\alpha = \mathrm{int}$, then $\beta = \mathrm{bool}$.'' These arise from conditional expressions and pattern matching. The standard approach is to defer such constraints, solving only the unconditional ones and checking deferred constraints against the partial solution.

\begin{definition}[Conditional Constraint]\label{def:conditional-constraint}
A \emph{conditional constraint} has the form $c \Rightarrow (s = t)$, meaning ``if constraint $c$ is satisfied, then $s$ must unify with $t$.'' A constraint system with conditional constraints is solved by first solving the unconditional fragment, then checking each conditional constraint under the partial solution.
\end{definition}

