\section{Type Inference for Simple Types}\label{sec:simple-type-inference}

With constraint generation and unification in hand, we can now present a complete type inference algorithm for the simply-typed lambda calculus.

\subsection{Algorithm Overview}\label{sec:inference-overview}

The type inference algorithm takes an untyped term $e$ and either returns a type $\tau$ (the most general type of $e$) or reports a type error. It proceeds as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Assign a fresh type variable to each bound variable and each subexpression.
  \item Generate constraints by traversing the term.
  \item Solve the constraints using unification.
  \item Read off the type from the solution.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Type Inference for the Simply-Typed Lambda Calculus}\label{alg:type-inference}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{InferType}{$\Gamma, e$}
  \State $\tau, C \gets \mathrm{Generate}(\Gamma, e)$
  \State $\sigma \gets \mathrm{Unify}(C)$
  \If{$\sigma = \mathrm{fail}$}
    \State \Return \textbf{error} ``Type mismatch''
  \EndIf
  \State \Return $\sigma(\tau)$
\EndFunction
\Statex
\Function{Generate}{$\Gamma, e$}
  \State \textbf{case} $e$ \textbf{of}
  \State \quad $x$: \Return $\Gamma(x), \emptyset$
  \State \quad $\lambda x.\, e'$:
    \State \quad $\alpha \gets \mathrm{fresh}()$
    \State \quad $\tau, C \gets \mathrm{Generate}(\Gamma[x \mapsto \alpha], e')$
    \State \quad \Return $\alpha \to \tau, C$
  \State \quad $e_1\; e_2$:
    \State \quad $\tau_1, C_1 \gets \mathrm{Generate}(\Gamma, e_1)$
    \State \quad $\tau_2, C_2 \gets \mathrm{Generate}(\Gamma, e_2)$
    \State \quad $\alpha \gets \mathrm{fresh}()$
    \State \quad \Return $\alpha, C_1 \cup C_2 \cup \{\tau_1 = \tau_2 \to \alpha\}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Principal Types}\label{sec:principal-types}

A central result in type inference theory is the existence of \emph{principal types}: types from which all other valid types can be obtained by substitution.

\begin{definition}[Principal Type]\label{def:principal-type}
A type $\tau$ is a \emph{principal type} for expression $e$ in environment $\Gamma$ if:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ (it is a valid type), and
  \item For every $\tau'$ such that $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau'$, there exists a substitution $\sigma$ such that $\sigma(\tau) = \tau'$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Principal Type Theorem for STLC]\label{thm:principal-type}
Every typable expression in the simply-typed lambda calculus has a principal type.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The constraint generation algorithm produces a finite set of type equations. The most general unifier, if it exists, yields the most general solution to these equations. Since the MGU is unique up to renaming, the resulting type is principal. \qed
\end{proof}

\subsection{Let-Bindings and Monomorphic Inference}\label{sec:let-monomorphic}

Without let-polymorphism (which we treat in \cref{ch:hm-inference}), let-bindings are typed monomorphically: the bound expression receives a single type, and the variable is used at that type throughout the body.

\begin{semrules}{Monomorphic Let}
  \typerule{Let}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 \rightsquigarrow \tau_1 \mid C_1 \quad
                  \Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e_2 \rightsquigarrow \tau_2 \mid C_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{let}\; x = e_1\; \mathtt{in}\; e_2 \rightsquigarrow \tau_2 \mid C_1 \cup C_2}
\end{semrules}

This restriction means that \texttt{let $x = \mathtt{id}$ in $(x\; 3, x\; \mathtt{true})$} fails to type-check, because $x$ cannot be simultaneously $\mathrm{int} \to \mathrm{int}$ and $\mathrm{bool} \to \mathrm{bool}$. The Hindley--Milner system of \cref{ch:hm-inference} lifts this restriction.

