\section{Bidirectional Type Checking}\label{sec:bidirectional}

Full type inference is undecidable for many useful type systems, including System~F (\cref{ch:poly-types}) and systems with subtyping (\cref{ch:subtyping}). \emph{Bidirectional type checking}, introduced by Pierce and Turner~\cite{pierce1998}, offers a principled compromise: the programmer provides type annotations at strategic points, and the checker infers types everywhere else.

\subsection{The Two Modes}\label{sec:two-modes}

Bidirectional type checking defines two mutually recursive judgments:

\begin{itemize}
  \item $\Gamma \vdash e \Rightarrow \tau$ (\textbf{inference}): ``Given $\Gamma$, we can determine that $e$ has type $\tau$.''
  \item $\Gamma \vdash e \Leftarrow \tau$ (\textbf{checking}): ``Given $\Gamma$ and a type $\tau$, we can verify that $e$ has type $\tau$.''
\end{itemize}

The key insight is that some constructs are naturally suited to inference (variables, applications with annotated functions), while others are naturally suited to checking (lambdas, let-bindings with annotated bound variables).

\begin{semrules}{Bidirectional Type Checking}
  \typeaxiom{Var-Check}{\Gamma \vdash x \Leftarrow \tau \quad \text{if } \Gamma(x) = \tau} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs-Check}{\Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e \Leftarrow \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x.\, e \Leftarrow \tau_1 \to \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Abs-Infer}{\Gamma, x : \tau_1 \vdash e \Leftarrow \tau_2}
            {\Gamma \vdash \lambda x : \tau_1.\, e \Rightarrow \tau_1 \to \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{App-Infer}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 \Rightarrow \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \quad
                        \Gamma \vdash e_2 \Leftarrow \tau_1}
            {\Gamma \vdash e_1\; e_2 \Rightarrow \tau_2} \\[6pt]
  \typerule{Subsume}{\Gamma \vdash e \Rightarrow \tau' \quad \tau' = \tau}
            {\Gamma \vdash e \Leftarrow \tau}
\end{semrules}

\subsection{Subsumption and Subtyping}\label{sec:subsumption}

When the type system includes subtyping (\cref{ch:subtyping}), the subsumption rule becomes:

\[
  \typerule{Subsume-Sub}{\Gamma \vdash e \Rightarrow \tau' \quad \tau' <: \tau}
            {\Gamma \vdash e \Leftarrow \tau}
\]

This allows a value of a more specific type to be used where a more general type is expected. The interplay between inference, checking, and subsumption must be carefully designed to avoid ambiguity.

\subsection{Bidirectional Typing for Records and Variants}\label{sec:bidirectional-records}

Bidirectional typing extends naturally to record and variant types. For records, checking mode allows the field types to be inferred from the expected type:

\[
  \typerule{Record-Check}{\Gamma \vdash e_1 \Leftarrow \tau_1 \quad \cdots \quad \Gamma \vdash e_n \Leftarrow \tau_n}
            {\Gamma \vdash \{l_1 = e_1, \ldots, l_n = e_n\} \Leftarrow \{l_1 : \tau_1, \ldots, l_n : \tau_n\}}
\]

For variants, inference mode determines the variant type from the tag and the inferred type of the payload:

\[
  \typerule{Variant-Infer}{\Gamma \vdash e \Rightarrow \tau}
            {\Gamma \vdash \mathtt{inj}_{l}(e) \Rightarrow \langle l : \tau \mid \ldots \rangle}
\]

\subsection{Completeness and Annotation Placement}\label{sec:annotation-placement}

\begin{theorem}[Bidirectional Completeness]\label{thm:bidirectional-complete}
If $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ in the declarative system, then there exists an annotation of $e$ (adding type annotations at lambda-bound variables and let-bound variables) such that the annotated term is accepted by the bidirectional system with type $\tau$.
\end{theorem}

The theorem tells us that bidirectional checking is \emph{complete relative to annotation placement}: if the programmer provides enough annotations, the checker will accept all valid programs. The art of language design lies in choosing a minimal set of annotation sites that covers typical usage patterns.

\begin{insight}
Bidirectional type checking has become the de facto standard for languages with complex type systems. It is used in GHC Haskell (for type-class contexts), in Agda and Coq (for dependent types), in TypeScript (for structural subtyping), and in many other systems. Its practical success rests on a simple principle: the programmer and the compiler cooperate, each doing what they do best.
\end{insight}

