\section{Local Type Inference}\label{sec:local-type-inference}

\emph{Local type inference} (Pierce and Turner~\cite{pierce2000}) extends bidirectional checking with limited inference of type arguments to polymorphic functions. The idea is to infer type arguments at call sites from the types of value arguments, without requiring global unification.

\subsection{Type Argument Inference}\label{sec:type-arg-inference}

Consider a polymorphic function $\mathtt{map} : \forall \alpha.\, \forall \beta.\, (\alpha \to \beta) \to [\alpha] \to [\beta]$. When the programmer writes $\mathtt{map}\; f\; xs$, the type checker can infer $\alpha$ and $\beta$ from the types of $f$ and $xs$:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Infer the type of $f$: say $\mathrm{int} \to \mathrm{string}$.
  \item Infer the type of $xs$: say $[\mathrm{int}]$.
  \item Match against the expected parameter types: $\alpha = \mathrm{int}$, $\beta = \mathrm{string}$.
\end{enumerate}

This is a form of \emph{local} inference: it uses only the types of immediately available expressions, not global constraint solving.

\subsection{Local Inference with Subtyping}\label{sec:local-subtyping}

When subtyping is present, local type inference must account for the fact that the argument types may be subtypes of the expected parameter types. Pierce and Turner's algorithm works by:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Inferring the types of all arguments.
  \item Computing the \emph{minimal} type instantiation that is compatible with the inferred argument types under subtyping.
  \item Checking that the result type is compatible with the expected type.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Limitations}\label{sec:local-limitations}

Local type inference cannot handle all cases. When a polymorphic function's type arguments cannot be determined from the value arguments alone (for example, $\mathtt{empty} : \forall \alpha.\, [\alpha]$), the programmer must provide explicit type annotations or use a more powerful inference mechanism.

\begin{example}[Failure of local inference]\label{ex:local-failure}
The expression $\mathtt{let}\; xs = \mathtt{empty}\; \mathtt{in}\; \mathtt{head}\; xs$ cannot be typed by local inference alone, because the type of $\mathtt{empty}$ is not constrained by any value arguments. The programmer must write $\mathtt{empty}\langle \mathrm{int} \rangle$ or provide a type annotation on $xs$.
\end{example}

