\section{Implementation Strategies}\label{sec:implementation-strategies}

The implementation of a type checker involves numerous engineering decisions that profoundly affect performance, error message quality, and maintainability.

\subsection{Representation of Types}\label{sec:type-representation}

Types are typically represented as trees or DAGs in memory. The choice of representation affects the cost of fundamental operations:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Type representation strategies and their trade-offs}\label{tab:type-repr}
\begin{tabular}{lccp{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Representation} & \textbf{Equality} & \textbf{Substitution} & \textbf{Notes} \\
\hline
Naive trees & $O(n)$ & $O(n)$ & Simple but wasteful \\
Hash-consed trees & $O(1)$ & $O(n)$ & Structural sharing via hash table \\
De Bruijn indices & $O(n)$ & $O(n)$ & Nameless; avoids $\alpha$-conversion \\
Nominal with caching & $O(1)$ amortized & $O(n)$ & Names with hash-consing \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Error Recovery and Reporting}\label{sec:error-recovery}

A type checker that aborts on the first error is of limited use. Production type checkers employ several strategies for error recovery:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Continuation after errors.} When a constraint fails, assign a fresh ``error type'' and continue checking, collecting all errors.
  \item \textbf{Hole filling.} When a type cannot be determined, introduce a metavariable and report it as an ``unresolved type'' at the end.
  \item \textbf{Error slicing.} For a chain of constraints leading to a contradiction, identify the minimal subset responsible and report only those locations.
  \item \textbf{Suggested fixes.} Use edit-distance or type-distance heuristics to suggest corrections for common mistakes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Incremental Type Checking}\label{sec:incremental}

Modern IDEs require type checking to be responsive---on the order of milliseconds for small edits. Incremental type checking achieves this by:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Maintaining a dependency graph of definitions.
  \item When a definition changes, invalidating only the constraints that depend on it.
  \item Re-solving only the affected constraint subsystem.
  \item Caching solutions for unchanged subsystems.
\end{enumerate}

