\subsection{Constraint Solvers in Practice}\label{sec:practical-solvers}

Production compilers use a variety of constraint-solving strategies:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{GHC (Haskell).} Uses a constraint monad that combines unification with type-class resolution. Constraints are solved in a fixed-point loop that interleaves unification with dictionary construction.
  \item \textbf{Rust.} Uses a region-based constraint solver for lifetime inference, combined with a trait solver for trait bounds. The solver operates in a ``selection then confirmation'' strategy.
  \item \textbf{TypeScript.} Uses a structural type checker with local inference and limited constraint solving. The solver handles conditional types and template literal types through a normalization procedure.
  \item \textbf{OCaml.} Uses Algorithm~W (see \cref{ch:hm-inference}) with a union-find structure for efficient unification.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Performance Considerations}\label{sec:performance}

The performance of type checking is dominated by three factors:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Unification cost.} For large programs with many type variables, unification can dominate. Path-compressed union-find structures reduce the amortized cost to nearly linear.
  \item \textbf{Substitution application.} Applying a substitution to a large type can be expensive if the type is represented as a tree. Hash-consing and lazy substitution (applying substitutions only when types are inspected) mitigate this.
  \item \textbf{Constraint explosion.} Some type system features (e.g., type families in GHC) can generate exponentially many constraints. Careful constraint simplification and early pruning are essential.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{historical}
The first practical type inference algorithm was developed by Hindley~\cite{hindley1969} and Milner~\cite{milner1978} for the ML language. Their algorithm, now known as Algorithm~W, combined unification with a clever treatment of let-bindings that enabled polymorphic recursion within a decidable framework. The constraint-based view was articulated later by Pottier and R{\'e}my~\cite{pottier2005}, who showed that it provides a clean separation of concerns and enables modular extensions to the core algorithm.
\end{historical}

