\subsection{Reducing a Type Error to an Irreducible Constraint Core}

Constraint generation can attach a source span and a stable identifier to
every equality. Once solving fails, a diagnostic pass can use these
identifiers to look for a small contradiction rather than blaming the last
equation processed. The input is a finite, unsatisfiable set of first-order
type equalities over finite types, with rigid symbols and flexible
metavariables distinguished. The solver must decide satisfiability
including the occurs check. Each trial starts with the original
metavariables unbound, or with a rollback snapshot from before solving.
Discarding equations from an already mutated unification graph is not
equivalent to solving a smaller system.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Unsatisfiable equalities $C$ in stable source order
\State $K\gets C$
\ForAll{equation IDs $i$ in the original $C$}
  \State $T\gets K\setminus\{i\}$
  \State $R\gets\Call{FreshSolver}{}$ \Comment{No retained substitutions}
  \If{\Call{Contradiction}{$R,T$}}
    \State $K\gets T$ \Comment{The omitted equation is unnecessary}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State collect the source spans of IDs in $K$
\State \Return $K$ and its spans
\end{sudocode}

For example, start with $\{\alpha=\mathrm{Int},
\beta=\mathrm{Bool},\alpha=\mathrm{Bool}\}$ in that order.
Removing the first equality makes the rest satisfiable, so the
pass keeps it. Removing the middle equality still leaves an
$\mathrm{Int}$--$\mathrm{Bool}$ contradiction and succeeds. Removing
the final equality from the reduced set leaves just
$\alpha=\mathrm{Int}$, which is satisfiable. The diagnostic can now
point to the two uses of $\alpha$ and avoid mentioning $\beta$.

\Cref{fig:visual-43} shows the equality path responsible for the
contradiction and why the middle equation can be discarded.

% Explanatory visual
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[tome diagram,x=1cm]
  \node[tome box] (int) at (0,0) {$\mathrm{Int}$};
  \node[ast node] (alpha) at (3.25,0) {$\alpha$};
  \node[tome box] (b1) at (6.5,0) {$\mathrm{Bool}$};
  \draw[thick] (int) -- node[above] {equation 1} (alpha);
  \draw[thick] (alpha) -- node[above] {equation 3} (b1);
  \draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=4pt}]
    (-1.05,0.92) -- (7.55,0.92) node[midway,above=5pt] {forces unequal rigid types equal};
  \node[ast node] (beta) at (3.25,-1.35) {$\beta$};
  \node[tome box] (b2) at (6.5,-1.35) {$\mathrm{Bool}$};
  \draw[dashed] (beta) -- node[above] {equation 2} (b2);
  \node[anchor=east] at (2.5,-1.35) {discarded};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The two drawn occurrences of $\mathrm{Bool}$ denote the
same rigid type. Equations 1 and 3 alone force $\mathrm{Int}=\mathrm{Bool}$.
Equation 2 constrains only $\beta$ and is not needed for the contradiction.}
\label{fig:visual-43}
\end{figure}

The working set stays unsatisfiable. Whenever an equation survives
its trial, deleting it gave a satisfiable set; subsequent deletions
cannot make that trial set unsatisfiable. Thus the final set is
inclusion-minimal, not necessarily minimum-cardinality: a different
source order might find another core. This method uses at most one
additional solver run per original equality. With a diagnostic time
budget, stopping early still gives an unsatisfiable core, but the
tool should not claim irreducibility. Any defaulting or conversion
rules that make dropping constraints nonmonotonic need a different
argument.

\section{Summary and Further Reading}\label{sec:summary-43}

This chapter has established the foundations of type checking and type inference. We have seen how type checking can be decomposed into constraint generation and constraint solving, how unification provides the core algorithm for solving type equations, and how bidirectional type checking offers a practical compromise between full inference and full annotation.

The Hindley--Milner system, presented in the next chapter, extends these ideas with let-polymorphism, enabling a form of parametric polymorphism that can be inferred without any programmer annotations. The chapters that follow explore increasingly sophisticated type systems---polymorphic, subtyping, dependent, linear---each of which presents new challenges and opportunities for type checking and inference.

For further reading, we recommend Pottier and R{\'e}my's comprehensive treatment of type inference~\cite{pottier2005}, Pierce and Turner's work on bidirectional typing~\cite{pierce1998}, and Dunfield and Krishnaswami's tutorial on bidirectional type checking~\cite{dunfield2021}.
